TITLE = "Trailer Hitch Classes Explained: Class 1 to 5, Receiver Sizes and Ratings"
META = ("Trailer hitch Classes 1–5 explained: receiver sizes, gross trailer and tongue weight ranges from CURT and etrailer, "
        "and why your vehicle's rating always wins.")

FAQ = [
 ("What are the five trailer hitch classes?",
  "Classes 1 through 5 group receiver hitches by capacity and receiver size. CURT lists Class 1 at up to 2,000 lb gross trailer weight and 200 lb tongue weight on a 1-1/4 in receiver, Class 2 at up to 3,500 lb and 350 lb on 1-1/4 in, Class 3 at up to 8,000 lb and 800 lb on 2 in, Class 4 at up to 10,000 lb and 1,000 lb on 2 in, and Class 5 from 16,000 lb on 2 in up to 20,000 lb on 2-1/2 in. Other makers publish slightly different ranges."),
 ("Does a Class 3 hitch mean I can tow 8,000 lb?",
  "No. 8,000 lb is the top of CURT's Class 3 range, not the rating of every Class 3 hitch and certainly not your vehicle's rating. Real Class 3 hitches in the site's guides are rated 3,500 lb (CURT 13493 for the Bronco) and 4,500 lb (Draw-Tite 76527, B&W for the RAV4). And both CURT and etrailer say the same thing: you are limited by the lowest-rated part of the setup. A 4,500 lb hitch on a RAV4 rated for 1,500 lb tows 1,500 lb."),
 ("What is the difference between a 1-1/4 in and a 2 in receiver?",
  "The opening size and, with it, the capacity. Class 1 and 2 hitches use a 1-1/4 in receiver and top out at 3,500 lb gross trailer weight and 350 lb tongue weight on CURT's chart. Class 3 and 4 hitches use a 2 in receiver. Most hitch bike racks and cargo carriers are made for 2 in, so a 2 in receiver gives you more accessory choice. Pick 1-1/4 in only if you already own 1-1/4 in gear or the vehicle only takes a small hitch."),
 ("How much tongue weight should a trailer have?",
  "etrailer's guidance is 10–15% of the loaded trailer's weight, so a 10,000 lb trailer should put 1,000–1,500 lb on the ball, and a 3,000 lb trailer 300–450 lb. NHTSA's towing guide explains why it matters: too little tongue weight can make the trailer sway, and too much takes weight off the tow vehicle's front wheels. The resulting figure must fit inside the hitch's tongue-weight rating and the vehicle's own tongue-weight limit from the owner's manual."),
 ("Can I use a 1-1/4 to 2 in adapter?",
  "You can, but it costs capacity. CURT says a 1-1/4 to 2 in adapter reduces the hitch or vehicle's tongue weight capacity by up to 50%, which leaves a Class 1 hitch at around 100 lb of tongue weight or less, and that towing capacity can drop 30% or more depending on the adapter and hitch. That is not enough for a loaded 2 in bike rack. Going the other way, etrailer notes that adapters let you carry Class I and II accessories on a larger hitch; the limit is then the accessory's own rating."),
 ("Where do I find my vehicle's towing capacity?",
  "Start with the owner's manual or the maker's towing guide, which list tow and tongue ratings by engine, drivetrain and tow package. Then check the labels on the driver's door jamb: the certification label gives the gross vehicle and axle weight ratings, and the tire and loading placard gives the most weight of people and cargo the vehicle should carry. Tongue weight counts against that payload. If the manual lists several ratings, use the one that matches your exact configuration."),
 ("When do I need a weight-distribution hitch?",
  "When the vehicle's owner's manual or the hitch's ratings call for one, and when the trailer is heavy enough to make the rear sag and the front go light. NHTSA's guide describes weight-distributing hitches as a receiver hitch plus special parts that spread tongue weight across all the tow vehicle's and trailer's axles. Only use one on a hitch rated for it: CURT's 13493 lists a weight-distribution rating, while the Reese 06192 Class 2 is not rated for weight distribution at all."),
 ("Do I need a 4-way or 7-way connector?",
  "A 4-way flat connector carries the basic lights (tail, turn and brake signals plus ground) and suits most small utility trailers, bike trailers and light boat trailers. A trailer with electric brakes needs a 7-way connector, which adds a brake circuit, a 12 V power feed and a reverse circuit, and a brake controller in the cab. Many vehicles take a plug-in, vehicle-specific harness, such as CURT's 56434 for the RAV4, which avoids splicing into factory wiring."),
 ("Do I need a brake controller?",
  "If the trailer has electric brakes, yes: the controller in the cab is what applies them. Requirements for trailer brakes are set by each state, often by trailer weight, so check your state's rules and your owner's manual. Oregon's reprint of NHTSA's towing guide, for example, says Oregon law does not require trailer brakes but strongly encourages them. Also check whether your vehicle has a factory controller; Bronco owners note that adding a hitch later does not add the tow package's controller."),
 ("Is a bike rack on a hitch the same as towing?",
  "No, a hitch rack is pure tongue weight: the rack and bikes hang on the receiver with no trailer axle carrying any of it. etrailer's example is a 200 lb rack with two 50 lb bikes, 300 lb in total, which can overload a small hitch. Compare the loaded rack weight to the hitch's tongue-weight rating and the vehicle's tongue limit, and remember that the further the load sits from the bumper, the more leverage it puts on the receiver."),
]

